-- Passage rg6-argument-evidence-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c5e1828e-c6a4-56cf-b252-02ad24ec8cdb', 'rg6-argument-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Keep the Library Open on Sunday Afternoons$q$, $q$The Hollis Public Library closes at noon on Sundays. Many families in our town are busy during the week, so Sunday afternoon is their only free time. The library should stay open until five o'clock on Sundays.

First, people want to use the library on weekends. Last year, the library counted visitors every hour. Sunday morning had more visitors per hour than any weekday morning. On most Sundays, people were still waiting at the door when staff locked up at noon.

Second, students need a quiet place to finish homework before the school week starts. Many projects are due on Monday. At the library, students can use the computers, the printer and the reference books for free. Not every family has these things at home.

Third, longer hours would help the library offer more programs. Right now, the craft club and the chess club meet on weekday evenings, when many children are at practice or lessons. Sunday afternoons would give these clubs a better time slot.

The library building is also the oldest in town, and its brick front is very beautiful.

Some people say that longer hours would cost too much. However, the library could use volunteers to help at the front desk, as the library in nearby Corwin does. Corwin added Sunday hours two years ago and reports that its costs went up only a small amount.

A library is only useful when its doors are open. Hollis should give its families more time to read, learn and gather on Sundays.$q$, 'draft')
