-- Passage rg6-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('eb1b33e5-e4ea-50ae-9236-f56df6d09c92', 'rg6-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Does Music Help Plants Grow?$q$, $q$Darnell and his classmate Yuki read a blog post claiming that playing music makes houseplants grow faster. They decided to test the idea themselves.

They bought six bean seedlings of the same height. They put three in a room where a radio played soft music all day. They put the other three in a quiet room down the hall. Each plant got the same amount of water every morning.

After three weeks, the plants in the music room were about two centimeters taller on average. At first, Darnell was sure the blog was right. Yuki was not so sure. She pointed out that the music room had a large window facing south, while the quiet room had only a small window. More sunlight, she said, could explain the difference just as well as the music.

The two students also noticed other problems. Six plants is a very small number. One unusually tall plant could change the average. And three weeks is a short time for a test.

So they planned a better experiment. This time they would use twenty plants, and both groups would sit side by side near the same window. Only one group would wear small headphones playing music. Everything else, from the soil to the water, would be the same.

The blog post had said that "everyone knows plants love music." Darnell and Yuki learned that what "everyone knows" is not the same as evidence. A claim needs a fair test before people should believe it.$q$, 'draft')
