   $q$Which detail shows why the first test's evidence was too weak to trust?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Darnell and Yuki read about the idea on a blog post.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"},{"text":"Only six plants were used, so one unusual plant could change the average.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The seedlings were all the same height at the very start.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misjudges-support"},{"text":"The radio in the music room played soft music all day.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misjudges-support"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Only six plants were used, so one unusual plant could change the average.$q$, $q$The passage says six plants is "a very small number" and "one unusually tall plant could change the average." Starting with plants of the same height actually made the test fairer, not weaker.$q$, 'draft', 'eb1b33e5-e4ea-50ae-9236-f56df6d09c92')
