-- Passage rg6-argument-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cbff0084-36ee-5e27-b4a1-e00be08cc3ea', 'rg6-argument-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Every Classroom Needs a Class Pet$q$, $q$Ms. Ferreira's fifth-grade class at Oak Ridge Elementary has a guinea pig named Pepper. I believe every classroom in our school should have a small pet like Pepper.

First, caring for a pet teaches responsibility. In Ms. Ferreira's class, students take turns feeding Pepper, filling her water bottle and cleaning her cage. A chart on the wall shows whose job it is each day. Ms. Ferreira says that in three years, Pepper has never missed a meal.

Second, a class pet can make students feel calm. When I visited the class, a boy who was upset about a test sat quietly near the cage for a few minutes. Afterward, he went back to work. Pets are the most relaxing thing in the whole world.

Third, a pet can be part of science lessons. Students in Ms. Ferreira's class weigh Pepper every Friday and graph her weight. They also read about what guinea pigs eat and why their teeth never stop growing.

Some teachers worry about students with allergies. This is a fair point. Schools could choose pets that are less likely to cause allergies, such as fish or certain lizards, and keep them in closed tanks. Teachers could also check with families before a pet arrives.

Other people say pets are too much work for busy teachers. But in Ms. Ferreira's class, the students do almost all of the work themselves.

A class pet turns a classroom into a small community. Every class at Oak Ridge deserves one.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
