-- Passage rg6-argument-evidence-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d2924bae-388e-50cc-95eb-a4a6bff6589a', 'rg6-argument-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Walking School Bus$q$, $q$In many towns, children walk to school together in a group led by adult volunteers. This is called a walking school bus. The group follows a set route and picks up children at "stops" along the way, just like a real bus. Pinewood Elementary should start a walking school bus this year.

One reason is that walking is healthy. Doctors recommend that children get about an hour of activity each day. A fifteen-minute walk to school and back gives students half an hour before they even reach the playground.

Another reason is that walking in a group is safer than walking alone. The adult leaders wear bright vests and help children cross streets. In the town of Maple Falls, parents said they felt comfortable letting their children walk once the walking bus began.

A walking school bus would also reduce traffic near the school. Each morning, a long line of cars waits to drop off students on Elm Street. The Pinewood parent group counted 140 cars in twenty minutes on a single Tuesday. If even a few dozen students walked instead, that line would shrink.

Finally, children who walk to school arrive happier and get better grades than children who ride in cars.

Some parents point out that not every family lives close enough to walk. That is true. The walking bus could start from a meeting spot, such as the park on Birch Road, so families who drive could drop children there.

Pinewood has sidewalks, willing parents and many students who live nearby. It has everything it needs to get started.$q$, 'draft')
